398:
Just as in the dyads (dukas), in the triads (tikas) as well, by making 49 questions for each triad, in all 22 triads:
There are one thousand and seventy-eight questions;
In the triad-root method (tika-mūlaka-naya), the questions are thus by count.

Beyond that, the four-root method (catu-mūlaka-naya) is commenced as: root condition (hetu-paccaya), object condition (ārammaṇa-paccaya), predominance condition (adhipati-paccaya), and proximity condition (anantara-paccaya). In that four-root method, together with the first triad, there are 21 tetrads (catukkas) by way of combining each of the 21 conditions starting from proximity condition. Among those tetrads, two tetrads are shown and the remaining tetrads are summarized. Here too, by making 49 questions for each tetrad, in all 21 tetrads:
There are one thousand and twenty-nine questions;
In the four-root method (catu-mūlaka-naya), the questions are thus by count.

Beyond that, the teaching is performed starting from the five-root (pañca-mūlaka) up to the all-root (sabba-mūlaka). Summarizing all that, and combining the method already stated below with the method to be stated above, the method is shown in the Pāḷi text as: "The one-root, two-root, triad-root, four-root, five-root, and all-root should be expanded by one who is not confused." Regarding those, what should be said for the one-root etc. has already been said. In the five-root method, however, together with the first tetrad, there are 20 pentads (pañcakas) by way of combining each of the 20 conditions starting from contiguity condition (samanantara-paccaya). By making 49 questions for each of those pentads:
There are nine hundred and eighty questions;
In the five-root method (pañca-mūlaka-naya), the questions are thus by count.

In the six-root (chakka-mūlaka), together with the first pentad, there are 19 hexads (chakkas) by way of combining each of the 19 conditions starting from conascence condition (sahajāta-paccaya). By making 49 questions for each of those hexads:
There are nine hundred and thirty-one questions;
In the six-root method (chakka-mūlaka-naya), the questions are thus by count.
